# Keep the build context small and, more importantly, keep secrets out of it.
# Anything copied into a layer is recoverable from the image even if a later
# layer deletes it.

# Secrets and local config -- the single most important entries here.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
jwt_private.pem
jwt_public.pem

# Version control and CI
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Local virtualenv -- would otherwise be copied in and shadow the image's own
.venv
venv
env

# Python build/cache artifacts
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.import_linter_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Tests and docs aren't needed at runtime
tests/
docs/
*.md
!README.md

# The admin console. It is a separate Next.js app with its own deployment and
# is never copied into this image -- but Docker sends the whole context to the
# daemon before any layer runs, so leaving it here meant `node_modules` (490
# packages, tens of thousands of files) and `.next` were transferred on *every*
# build. On Docker Desktop, where the context crosses a VM filesystem boundary,
# that is the difference between a slow build and an unusable one.
frontend/

# Local object storage -- real uploaded tenant documents when STORAGE__MODE=local.
# Both a size problem and a disclosure one: anything in the context can end up
# in a layer, and this directory is exactly the customer data the image must
# never carry.
var/

# Local development tooling
docker-compose*.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
.claude/

# NOT ignored: `scripts/`. The Dockerfile copies it deliberately (production
# bootstrap is `docker compose run --rm migrate python scripts/...`, see
# DEPLOYMENT.md), and excluding it here made `COPY scripts ./scripts` fail
# with "/scripts: not found" -- i.e. the production image could not be built
# at all. The COPY was added in a later session than the last image build, so
# nothing caught it until the image was rebuilt.

# Editor/OS noise
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
